How are containers isolated from each other?

First, namespaces give the process its own view of the system.

And cgroups allow us to control the resources.

Let's talk about what a container is.

It is an isolated environment where one or more processes can run.

Why do we call it a container?

In Arabic, container means "7awiya". It is like you take a process and give it its own world.

Why Docker?

Because managing a process and giving it its own world, or managing multiple processes, is kind of hard. This is why we use Docker.

You can manage all of that using commands.

We talked about containers, but we didn't mention images.

An image is like a template or a blueprint of a container. With one image, we can create multiple containers.

And what is inside this image?

It contains everything needed to create a container, such as the filesystem and environment.

Alert: CMD and ENTRYPOINT are Dockerfile instructions.

To run Docker without sudo, I need to add the user to the Docker group with:

`sudo usermod -aG docker $USER`

If I want to install an image from Docker Hub:

`docker pull image-name`

To see running containers:

`docker ps`

To see all containers, including stopped ones:

`docker ps -a`

If I want to run a container:

`docker run image-name`

If I want to run a container in the background:

`docker run -d image-name`

Docker will generate a container name automatically.

If I want to set a name myself:

`docker run --name set-name image-name`

If I want to stop a container:

`docker stop container-name`

or:

`docker stop container-id`

If I want to map a port:

`docker run -p 8080:80 image-name`

If I want to delete a container:

`docker rm container-name`

If I want to force-delete a running container:

`docker rm -f container-name`

If I want to start an existing container:

`docker start container-name`

or:

`docker start container-id`

If I want a new container, I use:

`docker run`

If I want to delete an image:

`docker rmi image-name`

If I want to run a command inside an existing container:

`docker exec container-name executable command`

For example:

`docker exec container-name ls`

If I want to interact with a shell process, I add `-it`.

Remember: `docker exec` starts a process inside an existing container.

If I want to see the logs of my container:

`docker logs container-name`

If I want detailed information about the container:

`docker inspect container-name`

In a Dockerfile:

We use:

`FROM debian:bookworm`

It means we begin from the Debian userspace.

The `RUN` instruction executes a command while building the image.

`CMD` is the command that runs when a container starts. It normally becomes the main process of the container. If this process finishes, the container also stops.

If we have `CMD` in the Dockerfile and we do:

`docker run image-name command`

then this command replaces the `CMD` and becomes the main process.

With `ENTRYPOINT`, you specify the main program or main process that runs when you start the container.

Other commands are passed as arguments to the main process.

If you want the other command to behave like a command passed to a shell, you add `-c`.

`COPY` is used when you want to copy a file from your host machine to a specific path in the image filesystem.
